A writer can accept a change before a reader can see it in object storage. Give those two promises different names.

[Dillon Mulroy](https://github.com/dmmulroy) built [ShaleDB](https://github.com/dmmulroy/shaledb), a log-structured store on Cloudflare Durable Objects, R2 and Workflows, written with Effect and Alchemy. It is a clear worked example of this split, and its design notes, invariants and measurements are worth reading in full.

Its [writer commits each batch to SQLite][lore-write]. The batch is durable at that point. It reaches object storage later.

## Publish one complete cut

A read cut marks which changes a reader can see. A manifest lists the objects that serve that cut.

1. Write the new data objects.
2. [Publish the manifest][lore-write] that names them. Refuse a conflicting publication.
3. Mark the covered part of the write log as eligible for cleanup.

A crash before publication can leave an unused object. The local log still has the writes. A crash after publication can leave extra log entries. The published cut still tells the reader what is available.

## Tell readers what they can see

A [published read][lore-read] can skip the writer. It can miss changes that have not reached the manifest. A fresh read checks the writer for those changes.

A [checkpoint](/lore/checkpoints-follow-commits) names an older cut. [A fork can share that cut's objects][lore-fork] if it first takes a hold on them. Releasing the hold is safe only when no permitted reader still needs the old objects.

## Prove the gap before adding a store

Stop the writer after it uploads data and before it publishes. Start it again. Check that every acknowledged write remains recoverable.

Repeat the test after publication and before cleanup. Then try a read at an older cut while cleanup runs. Check that the reader gets a complete answer or a clear expiry error.

[Compare with a plain SQLite table][lore-measure] before you add a manifest. Record the source revision, payload, read mix, and consistency mode. The extra machinery needs a job, such as keeping published reads off the writer or sharing a pinned cut.

